    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d40">

                <pb facs="0091" n="81" sameAs="frus:frus1864p1/pg81"/>

                <head>Mr. Seward to Mr.
                        Adams.</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 813.]</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Department of State,</hi>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Washington,</hi></placeName>
                        <date calendar="gregorian" when="1864-01-14"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >January</hi> 14, 1864.</date></dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir:</hi> Your despatch of December 24 (No. 563) has
                    been received, together with its voluminous and effective supplement of proofs
                    of the abuse of the British and international laws in the case of the
                    Rappahannock. The President does not permit himself to doubt that her Majesty’s
                    government will promptly apply in that case the remedies which are within its
                    power. Unfortunately the special remedies in single cases must necessarily fail
                    to remove an evil that has its source in a general policy that is radically
                    erroneous and anomalous.</p>
                <p>I am, sir, your obedient servant,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>WILLIAM H. SEWARD.</signed></closer>
                <postscript>
                    <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Charles F. Adams</hi>, Esq., <hi
                            rendition="#italic">&amp;c., &amp;c</hi>., <hi rendition="#italic"
                            >&amp;c.</hi></p>
                </postscript>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
